9.text:
The two inbred parental strains have fixed, complementary haplotypes at the five SNPs, denoted \(00000\) and \(11111\). Because every meiosis produces exactly one crossover, every gamete that gives rise to the F2 generation is the product of a single crossover between these two pure haplotypes. With four intervals lying between the five SNPs, this yields exactly eight distinct F2 haplotypes (four of the form \(0^k1^{5-k}\) and four of the form \(1^k0^{5-k}\)).

An F3 chromosome is likewise produced by a single crossover, but now the two parental chromosomes in the F2 individual are themselves drawn from the eight recombinant haplotypes above. Any such meiosis therefore superimposes one additional crossover on an already singly-recombinant background. The resulting F3 molecules therefore contain at most two crossovers and consequently display at most two allelic transitions.

All binary strings of length five that contain zero, one or two transitions are enumerated as follows:

- 0 transitions: \(00000\), \(11111\) (2 strings);
- 1 transition: the eight haplotypes already listed above (8 strings);
- 2 transitions: six strings that begin with 0 (pattern 010) and six that begin with 1 (pattern 101), for a total of 12 strings.

Hence exactly \(2+8+12=22\) distinct sequences are reachable. Every one of them can be realized by an appropriate choice of F2 parents and an appropriate placement of the second crossover, and none of the remaining ten sequences (those possessing three or more transitions) can appear under the single-crossover-per-meiosis constraint.

