6.text:
The two parental strains have fixed complementary haplotypes at the five SNP sites (denoted without loss of generality \(00000\) and \(11111\)). Each meiosis imposes exactly one crossover, so every chromosome transmitted through the F1-to-F2 generation is one of the eight single-transition strings obtained by placing that crossover in any of the four intervals and choosing either parental origin.

An F2 individual therefore carries a diploid pair drawn from those eight strings. Formation of an F3 gamete again imposes exactly one crossover, now between the two resident F2 chromosomes. The resulting mosaic is assembled by taking a prefix of one chromosome and the complementary suffix of the other at any of the four intervals. Consequently every F3 haplotype contains at most two crossovers and therefore exhibits at most two allelic transitions.

All binary strings of length five that satisfy this constraint are:

- 0 transitions: the two constant (parental) strings;
- 1 transition: the eight single-transition strings already enumerated for the F2 generation;
- 2 transitions: \(\binom{4}{2}=6\) ways to choose the pair of intervals, each admitting two possible starting alleles, for a total of 12 strings.

Enumeration therefore yields exactly \(2+8+12=22\) distinct sequences. Every one of them is attainable by an appropriate choice of F2 parents and crossover site; the three- and four-transition strings that complete the \(2^5=32\) possible binary strings require at least three crossovers and are unreachable.

The strand-asymmetry condition merely fixes the reference orientation; it does not further restrict the admissible set.

