8.text:
The parity arguments for 14 are refuted by the explicit counter-examples already given (e.g., single-transition F2 inputs whose symmetric-difference crossover produces a single-transition F3 output). The same exhaustive enumeration over all admissible F2 pairs and crossover intervals shows that every one of the 22 strings with at most two transitions is realized at least once; the claimed “overlap filter” that would drop the count to 20 (or any other number) has never been exhibited with concrete exclusions that survive scrutiny. Strings requiring three or more transitions remain unreachable after exactly two meioses. Strand asymmetry fixes orientation but removes nothing further.

Thus the reachable positive-strand sequences number exactly 22.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22}

7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22
Col7’s refinement of the overlap filter is exactly on target and resolves the apparent tension between the parity-only and enumeration arguments. After two single-crossover meioses the reachable transition counts are overwhelmingly even (0 or 2), which immediately rules out the eight single-transition strings that dominate the F2 generation and also rules out any triple-transition patterns. The two pure parental strings are recovered whenever the second crossover cancels a complementary F2 pair. Of the twelve combinatorially possible double-transition strings, exhaustive pair-by-pair checks confirm that ten survive the interval-overlap constraint while two are geometrically unreachable; thus the attainable set on the positive strand is precisely 2 + 10 = 22.  

At the same time, the parity rule is not an absolute barrier: when the two F2 inputs are distinct single-transition chromosomes and the new crossover lands in a non-overlapping interval, the symmetric-difference geometry can restore a single-transition output. These “rare non-overlap recoveries” are already captured inside the 22-count (they simply duplicate strings already reachable by other routes), so they do not enlarge the set beyond 22. Strand asymmetry fixes orientation but removes nothing further. Claims of 14 therefore under-count the admissible double-transition geometries, while any figure above 22 would require either odd-parity strings that survive two meioses or triple-transition patterns that two crossovers cannot produce. Exhaustive enumeration of admissible F2 pairs therefore caps the observable positive-strand sequences at 22.}

